        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Pick <strong class="text-white">opacity for your wood's condition</strong>, then a color that <strong class="text-white">coordinates with your home</strong> and suits your sun exposure. Cedar and medium browns are the safe, flattering defaults; gray and driftwood are the modern move. Whatever you choose, test it on the actual deck first — it's the cheapest insurance against a color you'll stare at for the next five years.
